/* Wealth Lab · Module 10 Academic Portfolio Design and Risk · 2026-11-02 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM10Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m10-academic-v1","version":"3.0.0","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","lessonIds":["L10.S1","L10.S2","L10.S3","L10.S4"],"lessons":[{"id":"L10.S1","title":"Asset Allocation and Goal Buckets","subtitle":"Strategic allocation, liabilities, funding ratios and portfolio loss capacity","prerequisites":["L1.S1 liquid assets and liabilities","L7.S3 present and future values","L9.S2 cash protection and dated securities"],"objectives":["Construct an asset/liability-aware policy instead of a one-size allocation","Differentiate financial risk capacity from behavioral willingness","Calculate portfolio asset weights, funding ratios and downside amounts","Separate emergency, purchase and long-horizon wealth funding","Explain strategic versus tactical asset allocation and glide paths","Stress real cash flows and sequence risk before using expected returns"],"sections":[{"title":"Allocation begins with the family's liabilities and dates, not its favorite fund","body":["A portfolio is not merely a list of securities. It is a financing arrangement for spending, emergencies, retirement and inheritance. Before selecting an asset mix, identify essential monthly outflows, contractual debt payments, near-term purchase commitments and future beneficiaries. The same person can rationally hold accessible insured deposits for a home closing and diversified equities for a retirement goal twenty-five years away. Those are not contradictory risk preferences; they are different liabilities supported by different pools of capital.","Asset allocation divides an investable pool among economic categories such as equity, fixed income, cash and possibly other exposures. Strategic allocation is the long-run mix chosen to meet objectives under constraints; tactical allocation temporarily changes exposure based on forecasts and introduces additional timing risk. The asset label is not enough: a long-duration bond fund may fall sharply before cash is needed, while a concentrated equity fund may increase employer-sector risk. The household must look through wrappers and account labels.","Suppose $40,000 of genuinely long-term investable assets are allocated 70% stocks, 20% bonds and 10% liquid holdings. Dollar targets are $28,000, $8,000 and $4,000. This arithmetic is straightforward but says nothing about suitability. If the family also owes a separate $12,000 mortgage closing payment in six months, including that money in the $40,000 equity portfolio creates a funding mismatch. The calculation must be repeated after protected near-term resources are removed.","An academic allocation memorandum connects each security to the cash flow it serves, states an expected time of withdrawal, defines how much temporary loss the household could tolerate, and documents the instrument's liquidity and taxes. The SEC emphasizes that allocation depends on time horizon and risk tolerance; the CFA curriculum also requires risk capacity, liquidity, constraints and a documented policy. Neither source endorses a universal portfolio percentage for every household."],"worked":["Long-term capital $40,000 ×70%=$28,000 stocks.","Bond target 20%×$40,000=$8,000; cash target 10%=$4,000.","Separate $12,000 closing obligation must have its own dated funding.","A portfolio screenshot showing $52,000 cannot claim $52,000 unrestricted risk capacity when $12,000 is contractually needed.","Under a 30% stock shock and zero change elsewhere, 70% stock exposure produces 21% portfolio loss before costs."],"practice":[["Why is a 70/20/10 allocation not a complete recommendation?","A percentage mix omits goal dates, available reserve, liabilities, household risk capacity, specific holdings, tax treatment and the loss that would threaten essential spending."],["Why can the same household properly maintain a conservative home fund and an equity-heavy retirement account?","The two objectives have different due dates and loss-absorption capacity; matching risk to liabilities is more meaningful than selecting one risk appetite for every dollar."]]},{"title":"Risk capacity, risk tolerance and required return are separate constraints","body":["Risk capacity is the financial ability to endure a loss without derailing essential goals. Risk willingness or tolerance is the emotional and behavioral readiness to remain invested through volatility. A person might be comfortable seeing a 40% market loss yet have no financial capacity for it because a mortgage closing is imminent. Another may have a secure pension and ample assets but panic during a 10% drawdown. A suitable plan generally cannot assume away the more restrictive constraint, and a questionnaire score is no substitute for actual cash-flow evaluation.","Required return is the return needed to fund a future objective under stated contributions and inflation, not an offer the market is obliged to provide. If the family needs $20,000 in twenty-four months from zero and can save only $500 at each month-end, it contributes $12,000. A model implying extraordinary return to reach the target is evidence the savings plan is underfunded or the deadline must change, not a reason to leverage speculative securities. Objectives may require changes in spending, timing or income rather than more portfolio risk.","A loss budget makes capacity concrete. A $30,000 long-term portfolio with maximum tolerable paper loss $6,000 has a stated 20% portfolio drawdown budget, but real correlations may make outcomes worse. If 60% of this portfolio is in equities and equities decline 35% while the rest stays flat, total loss is $6,300, already above the nominal threshold. The investor should reduce exposure or revise the constraint before committing funds, not after the crash.","Assess job stability, income concentration, debt service, disability coverage, minimum cash reserve, available credit obligations and household dependents. A mortgage buyer whose income and home equity both depend on one regional business cycle has less effective diversification than the brokerage asset chart suggests. Use probability estimates with caution; the real value of risk planning is knowing what action to take when assumptions fail."],"worked":["Long-term portfolio $30,000 with stated loss budget $6,000 ⇒ 20% tolerable decline.","60% of $30,000=$18,000 equities; equity shock −35% ⇒ −$6,300.","Other holdings unchanged ⇒ portfolio decline 21%, above stated budget.","Goal $20,000 due after twenty-four $500 contributions ⇒ only $12,000 deposits before returns.","No forecast return is a guaranteed remedy for an arithmetic funding gap."],"practice":[["Why is emotional willingness to tolerate stock volatility insufficient to justify a risky home fund?","Willingness does not create the cash needed for a dated liability; financial capacity depends on obligations, cash buffers and ability to recover from losses."],["What should a planner do if the investment return required to reach a near-term home target appears unrealistic?","Reassess target, contribution amount, time horizon or household resources, rather than disguise an unfunded goal with an unsupported high-return assumption."]]},{"title":"Three-bucket architecture: emergency, dated spending and long-horizon wealth","body":["Buckets are a useful way to assign ownership and permitted risks to money before investing it. An emergency bucket supports surprise essential bills and usually needs immediate accessibility. A dated spending bucket covers known or likely cash obligations such as a down payment, tuition or tax payment, with securities maturing before payment deadlines if suitable. A long-horizon bucket can accept market volatility when sufficient time and savings capacity exist. Bucket naming is not a special investment product or a guarantee of return.","Start with a household that has $18,000 total accessible cash, a $6,000 emergency minimum and $12,000 planned home closing funds. These two goals consume the entire $18,000. The presence of a separate brokerage account showing $5,000 in stocks should not lead the planner to count the same $5,000 twice unless that account genuinely is surplus to the stated obligations. An illiquid annuity with surrender penalties cannot be treated as an immediately accessible emergency reserve merely because it has an account value.","Buckets should be operationally mapped to actual account and transfer rules. Identify insured bank deposit limits, securities settlement cycles, available withdrawal dates, tax treatment, transfer holds and access by a partner or trustee under relevant legal terms. A savings balance visible on a phone is not necessarily liquid at the hour a closing attorney needs a certified payment. Different bucket investments may require different counterparties and records.","An allocation policy should specify how to replenish reserves after unexpected spending. If a $2,500 car repair reduces an $18,000 protected cash pool to $15,500, that cash shortfall must be reflected before new long-term investments are made. The reserve cannot remain fictional $18,000 in the plan while the bank balance has fallen. Goal segmentation is an accounting discipline as much as a behavioral tool."],"worked":["Starting accessible cash $18,000, emergency reserve $6,000 and closing fund $12,000.","Unassigned investable surplus $18,000−$6,000−$12,000=$0.","Unexpected essential repair $2,500 reduces total pool to $15,500.","If goal amounts are unchanged, reserve shortfall becomes $2,500.","Funding shortfalls should be disclosed and replenished before assuming speculative surplus."],"practice":[["Why should a brokerage position with a volatile market price not automatically be placed in the emergency bucket?","A required sale can coincide with a drawdown, settlement delay or tax event, so current market value may not become reliable immediate bill-paying cash."],["How should a funding plan change after a documented $2,500 emergency expense from fully allocated savings?","Reduce recorded accessible funds, identify which bucket was drawn down and establish a feasible replenishment plan rather than double counting the original balance."]]},{"title":"Funding ratios, present value and the liability-aware balance sheet","body":["Many investment presentations focus only on asset growth, but households have financial liabilities and future expense targets. The funding ratio compares available qualifying assets with the present value or scheduled face amount of a defined obligation, using consistent units. For cash due in six months, the useful practical measure may be immediately available dollar funding rather than discounted long-run expected portfolio value. Retirement plans often use present values based on actuarial and discount assumptions, making the ratio sensitive to rates and life expectancy.","Imagine a ten-year objective with annual end-year $10,000 payments for five years beginning in year six. At a hypothetical 4% effective annual discount rate, the present value is the sum of cash flows in years six to ten; it cannot simply be listed as $50,000 available today. A household holding $35,000 liquid assets toward this goal may or may not be fully funded depending on discount, inflation, risk, contributions and other assets. Use an auditable timeline rather than one scalar 'goal progress' number.","Another household plans a $20,000 house reserve due in two years and has $12,000 dedicated bank savings now. A simple undiscounted funded fraction is 60%. If it saves $250 each month for 24 months, new contributions total $6,000, bringing nominal dedicated funds to $18,000 before yields and taxes, still below $20,000. A market forecast cannot be counted as a guaranteed $2,000 bridge. A decision might require $333.33 monthly new contributions instead under a zero-return simplification.","Where objectives use real purchasing-power targets, increase the planned nominal bill under inflation scenarios or discount the portfolio into consistent real units. Housing market prices, down-payment requirements and closing fees may not rise at the same rate as CPI. A robust funded ratio has downside sensitivity and a plan for revising contributions, deadline or desired property cost."],"worked":["House goal $20,000, funded assets $12,000 ⇒ simple current funded ratio 60%.","Twenty-four monthly contributions ×$250=$6,000, nominal total $18,000 before growth.","Projected shortfall versus $20,000 target is $2,000 before returns and costs.","From $12,000 to $20,000 over 24 zero-return monthly deposits requires $8,000/24≈$333.33 monthly.","Future obligations and investment balances must be compared at matching dates and purchasing-power units."],"practice":[["Why must a funding ratio state the valuation date and which assets qualify as available?","A current stock price, a future expected investment return and an insured deposit have different cash dates, uncertainty and restrictions, so treating them identically distorts obligation coverage."],["How can a household respond to a $2,000 two-year home-fund forecast shortfall without increasing market risk?","Increase the affordable monthly contribution, modify the purchase budget or delay timing, or use verified eligible assistance; do not rely on a guaranteed speculative return."]]},{"title":"Strategic allocation, tactical tilts and the role of expected returns","body":["Strategic asset allocation is a rules-based target informed by the investor's return objective, risk capacity, available assets and time horizon. Tactical tilts deviate from that target based on changing expected valuations or economic forecasts; they can earn extra returns in favorable conditions but also create timing, tax and implementation risks. A portfolio holding equities because of a diversified twenty-year plan is different from moving all cash to stocks because a commentator predicts a rally next month.","Expected portfolio return in a simple one-period model is the weighted sum of component expected returns. If stocks have an illustrative 7% expectation, bonds 3% and cash 2%, a 60/30/10 mix has expected return 0.6×7%+0.3×3%+0.1×2%=5.3%. This is an arithmetic expectation under assumed inputs, not a forecast that next year's account must grow by 5.3%. Risk and correlation determine the spread around that expectation.","Costs matter in strategic and tactical allocation. A 1% portfolio-level advisory and fund-fee drag may materially reduce long-run compounding. Repeated tactical changes can generate taxable realized gains, bid-ask spreads and opportunity cost while the investor is out of the market. A forecast must be evaluated after expenses and compared with a passive strategic baseline on the same risk basis, rather than just select the strategy's best historical window.","Investment policy should name permitted ranges around targets and evidence needed to change strategic weights. An investor may reasonably reduce equity exposure when a home goal approaches or risk capacity changes; that is not market timing if the change follows a known liability. Altering target weights because of daily social-media price predictions is a different and much less reliable strategy."],"worked":["Target stocks 60%×7% expected=4.2 percentage points.","Target bonds 30%×3% expected=.9 point; cash 10%×2%=.2 point.","Total illustrative arithmetic expected portfolio return=5.3%.","A 1% annual cost on $40,000 is $400 initially under simple unchanged-balance approximation.","The expected value does not bound downside or guarantee any annual result."],"practice":[["Why is a weighted expected return of 5.3% not a promised yearly investment outcome?","Expected returns are assumption-based averages over uncertain outcomes; realized market prices, correlations, fees and inflation can make actual one-year results very different."],["How does reducing equities as a fixed home deadline approaches differ from a tactical call predicting a market decline?","The first reflects lower financial loss capacity due to a dated obligation, while the second relies on uncertain market timing information."]]},{"title":"Glide paths, lifecycle changes and sequence risk","body":["A glide path changes strategic exposure as the time to an objective shrinks. A retirement plan can gradually adjust stock, bond and cash weights to avoid relying on long-run averages just before withdrawals begin. A home purchase fund might have a simpler policy: once the closing amount is realistically earmarked, progressively reduce price risk until the funds are available as cash on the required date. The correct glide path is based on liabilities and other income, not an automatic percentage linked only to age.","Sequence-of-returns risk means that losses early in a withdrawal period can do disproportionate damage because the owner must sell more assets to support spending. An account beginning with $100,000 and withdrawing $10,000 after a 30% loss has $60,000 remaining in this simplified scenario. A later 30% recovery raises it only to $78,000 before further withdrawals. A different ordering of identical returns and withdrawals can yield a different final amount even if the mathematical average return is the same.","The relevant risk is not simply account volatility but shortfall probability when cash is due. A retirement income portfolio can combine liquidity reserves with long-run growth assets and periodically refill reserves under defined conditions. However, a cash bucket does not create new capital; selling investments to refill it during a depressed market may still realize losses. Stress the total balance sheet across early loss, late loss, higher inflation and longer life expectancy.","A professional glide-path justification identifies withdrawal amounts, timing, inflation assumptions, pensions and Social Security, tax account access, healthcare contingency and bequest priorities. For a Connecticut household aiming to leave a home to a daughter, the plan should consider debt service and trustee cash requirements alongside the inheritance value, without treating an illiquid property as ready-to-spend cash."],"worked":["Retirement portfolio initial $100,000; first-year market shock −30% ⇒ $70,000.","Withdrawal $10,000 after the shock ⇒ $60,000 remaining.","Following +30% return ⇒ $60,000×1.30=$78,000 without later withdrawal.","Arithmetic reversal of a −30% market move requires a +42.857% gain to recover the original value before withdrawals.","Withdrawal order can materially change final wealth even for identical multi-year return sets."],"practice":[["Why is sequence risk especially serious near the beginning of retirement?","Early withdrawals after losses sell more shares and reduce the capital available for future recovery, so the order of returns and cash flows changes sustainable spending."],["Why should a long-term real estate inheritance plan include cash and mortgage servicing needs?","The property may carry liens, taxes, insurance and repairs; intended beneficiaries could need cash to preserve or sell it rather than merely receive title."]]},{"title":"Formal allocation rationale and a documented downside decision","body":["An academic asset allocation decision should be defendable before a market movement occurs. Define each goal's amount, date, purchasing-power assumption and loss tolerance. Show current qualified assets, expected contributions, minimum cash buffer and possible income interruptions. Assign an investment category to each pool only after those obligations are measurable. The chosen allocation need not maximize historical average return; it must survive the household's plausible adverse outcomes.","Consider $40,000 long-term investable capital at a proposed 70/20/10 mix, plus a fully segregated $18,000 emergency and home-closing cash pool. Under an equity shock of −35%, bonds −5% and cash unchanged, long-term assets become $28,000×.65+$8,000×.95+$4,000=$29,800, a 25.5% loss or $10,200. The protected $18,000 cash is not itself a hedge creating positive portfolio return; it serves another purpose and should not be described as available for risk contributions.","Develop three scenarios: ordinary, adverse market and simultaneous income interruption. In the adverse case, quantify how much liquid cash remains after a deductible and whether the closing can still occur. In the income case, reduce planned contributions rather than assume household deposits continue despite unemployment. In each scenario show the rule that triggers a decision: pause investing, replenish reserve, reduce discretionary spending or revise purchase timing.","Conclude with an allocation memo including rationale, holding limits, diversification, tax location, implementation fees, rebalancing rules and review cadence. An observer should be able to test the student's calculations and understand what new evidence would cause the policy to change. This lays the foundation for quantitative covariance, risk contributions and formal investment policy construction in the next sections."],"worked":["Long-run portfolio $40,000: stock $28,000, bond $8,000, cash $4,000.","Stress stocks −35% ⇒ $18,200; bonds −5% ⇒ $7,600; cash $4,000 unchanged.","End value $29,800, loss $10,200 or 25.5%.","Separate fully funded $18,000 closing and emergency cash stays outside long-run risk budget.","Change in household income can reduce future contributions independent of portfolio returns."],"practice":[["Why is including emergency cash in the equity portfolio denominator potentially misleading?","It may make percentage risk look smaller while exposing or double counting money needed for essential spending and a separate dated obligation."],["What makes a written allocation policy more robust than changing positions whenever the stock market falls?","Predefined goals, loss limits, cash buffers and review triggers encourage decisions based on actual household constraints rather than fear or short-term price predictions."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFA 2026 portfolio planning and construction","https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/basics-of-portfolio-planning-and-construction"],["Investor.gov Asset Allocation and Diversification","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"],["Investor.gov beginner guide to allocation and rebalancing","https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/general-resources/publications-research/info-sheets/beginners-guide-asset"],["CFPB home purchase funds and affordability","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/"],["Investor.gov Investor Goals","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-goals"],["CFA suitability and IPS","https://www.cfainstitute.org/standards/professionals/code-ethics-standards/standards-of-practice-iii-c"]],"assessment":{"title":"Three-bucket funding and strategic allocation investment committee","prompt":"For a fictional Connecticut household with $18,000 earmarked for a $6,000 immediate emergency reserve and $12,000 closing, assess whether any cash is available for long-term risk assets. For a separate $40,000 retirement portfolio, implement 70/20/10 allocation and compute stress under stocks −35%, bonds −5%. Compute target return in 60/30/10 with stock 7%, bonds 3%, cash 2% assumed expectations. Model twenty-four deposits of $250 toward a $20,000 target with $12,000 initially. Distinguish capacity from willingness, funding gaps, glide path, inflation and sequence risk.","rubric":["Liability mapping and cash segmentation (25%)","Allocation arithmetic and goal stress (25%)","Risk capacity and sequence analysis (20%)","Implementation and policy constraints (20%)","Primary sources and judgment under downside (10%)"]}},{"id":"L10.S2","title":"Diversification and Correlation","subtitle":"Covariance, portfolio variance, factor exposure, concentration and tail risk","prerequisites":["L7.S3 compounded returns and financial mathematics","L8.S1 issuer exposure and equity risk","L9.S3 fund holdings and look-through concentration"],"objectives":["Distinguish systematic and diversifiable risk","Compute return covariance, variance, standard deviation and correlation","Apply the two-asset portfolio risk formula accurately","Assess correlation shifts, market stress and covariance estimation error","Quantify hidden industry and income concentrations","Interpret Sharpe ratio and VaR limitations with nonnormal scenarios","Build a defensible asset-class correlation and shock matrix"],"sections":[{"title":"Diversification works through economic exposures, not ticker quantity","body":["Diversification reduces vulnerability to independent issuer failures by spreading ownership across distinct economic cash-flow sources. It cannot remove broad recessions, inflation surprises, systemic funding shocks or changes in required investment returns. A person who owns five funds tracking the same large-cap index may effectively hold one equity exposure multiplied across five wrappers. Looking through holdings and business cycles is necessary to judge whether a second security truly changes portfolio risk.","Two risks are commonly distinguished: systematic risk, associated with broad market or shared macroeconomic factors, and issuer-specific risk such as one company's fraud, product recall or bankruptcy. Adding securities can dilute issuer-specific loss if positions are not perfectly correlated, but company events may cluster within industries and geographic regions. Holding more positions always adds complexity and trading or tax administration; it does not guarantee an optimal exposure or positive market return.","Consider two funds, with $6,000 in fund A and $4,000 in fund B, both tracking exactly the same stock index. If that benchmark falls 30% and tracking differences are ignored, the $10,000 portfolio loses $3,000: its market exposure is still 100% equity. A different ticker name, expense ratio or share class does not create a negative correlation or an independent return source. By contrast, a cash reserve earmarked for a liability solves an actual separate risk because its purpose is access when market prices decline.","Academic diversification must identify holdings, sector weights, equity duration, fixed-income maturity and credit, currencies, leverage and employment links. The proper starting point is a portfolio map of asset drivers and dates, not simply counting mutual fund positions. Marketwide shocks can dominate all the other sources even when historical correlations were modest."],"worked":["Fund A $6,000 and Fund B $4,000, both exactly same index ⇒ 100% look-through equity exposure.","Underlying index −30% ⇒ combined portfolio $7,000 under simplifying assumptions.","Dollar loss −$3,000; different fund names do not avoid it.","A single-sector risk factor can affect many holdings simultaneously.","Diversification aims to manage distributions of loss, not assure profit."],"practice":[["Why do two ETFs tracking the identical index produce little marginal diversification?","Their underlying economic exposures are nearly the same, so the same market movement can affect both regardless of wrapper or ticker."],["Can issuer diversification remove a recession-wide stock market decline?","No. Broad market and macroeconomic risks remain and can affect large groups of companies together, particularly during stressed funding conditions."]]},{"title":"Expected value, variance and covariance definitions","body":["A quantitative portfolio model describes uncertain returns using random variables. Expected return is the probability-weighted average of modeled returns, not a guaranteed realized result. Variance measures average squared deviation from an expected return; standard deviation is the positive square root of variance and shares the return unit. Covariance measures whether deviations in two returns move together, while correlation standardizes covariance by the product of the two standard deviations and ordinarily lies between −1 and +1.","For two securities A and B with possible economic states, expected return is Σp_s R_s. Covariance is Σp_s(R_A,s−μ_A)(R_B,s−μ_B) under a population state-probability framework. Historical sample covariance requires a different denominator convention and depends on the observation period and data cleaning. Switching between monthly and annual return standard deviations without rescaling can create serious risk errors. Percent returns should be converted consistently into decimal units before mathematical operations.","Suppose illustrative assets have expected returns 7% and 3%, annual standard deviations 20% and 8%, and annual correlation .25. Covariance is .25×.20×.08=.004 in decimal return-squared units. An investor reading the number .004 as 0.4% annual return would be confusing a squared statistical quantity with an expected monetary gain. The same covariance under positive volatility implies the assets often move partly together, but not always or in an exactly linear way.","Correlation is a historical or modeled descriptor, not a stable contractual fact. Returns can become much more correlated during a crisis, when investors sell multiple assets for liquidity. Covariance estimates based on a short calm period can understate the loss when stock and bond prices fall simultaneously. Use sensitivity intervals and stress scenarios rather than selecting one precise value as the permanent structure of financial markets."],"worked":["Expected stock return μA=7%, bond μB=3%.","Standard deviations σA=20%=.20, σB=8%=.08.","Correlation ρ=.25 ⇒ covariance=.25×.20×.08=.004 decimal-return squared.","Correlation can change without a change in asset name or legal classification.","Expected return, volatility and covariance must be given matching observation intervals."],"practice":[["Why is covariance not expressed in the same units as a simple annual percentage return?","Covariance multiplies deviations from two returns, producing a return-squared unit; converting it to correlation divides by two standard deviations."],["What is the danger of using only a short calm historical period to estimate asset correlations?","Stressed economic conditions may produce materially different correlations and volatilities, so the model can understate joint downside losses."]]},{"title":"Derive the two-asset portfolio risk equation","body":["With portfolio weights wA and wB summing to one, one-period portfolio return is wA RA+wB RB. Expanding the variance of that weighted sum yields σp²=wA²σA²+wB²σB²+2wAwBρσAσB. The covariance term makes portfolio risk depend on how assets move jointly, not merely their individual standard deviations. This fundamental equation also reveals why low or negative correlations can improve variance without necessarily increasing forecast return.","Use 60% in stocks and 40% bonds, with illustrative annual standard deviations 20% and 8%, and correlation .25. Stock variance contribution in the expansion is .6²×.2²=.0144, bond term .4²×.08²=.001024, and covariance cross term 2×.6×.4×.25×.20×.08=.00192. Total portfolio variance .017344, and standard deviation sqrt(.017344)≈13.1697%. Weighted average individual volatility 15.2% would be the wrong result for this correlation structure.","Change correlation to +1 while keeping the same weights and individual volatilities. Portfolio standard deviation becomes .6×20%+.4×8%=15.2%. If correlation drops toward zero or negative, modeled variance declines further. Yet lower standard deviation does not guarantee no losses. A portfolio can have fat-tailed returns and a substantial maximum drawdown while displaying modest ordinary-period standard deviation.","The result is a mathematical conditional model rather than a promise the market provides the assumed correlations. Portfolio volatility should be evaluated alongside cash shortfall probability, worst reasonable drawdown, concentration and asset duration. A household whose risk budget is a maximum $2,000 loss on a $12,000 closing fund should not assume a 13% annual standard deviation makes the investment date-safe."],"worked":["Weights 60% stocks, 40% bonds; annual σ=.20 and .08, correlation .25.","Stock term .6²×.2²=.0144; bond term .4²×.08²=.001024.","Cross term 2×.6×.4×.25×.20×.08=.00192.","Portfolio variance=.017344, volatility=sqrt(.017344)≈13.1697%.","Perfect positive correlation would give volatility 15.2% under unchanged assumptions."],"practice":[["Why does the formula contain a correlation term rather than just averaging the two individual volatilities?","Portfolio fluctuations depend on simultaneous asset deviations; covariance captures offsetting or reinforcing price movement across holdings."],["Does estimated 13.17% annual portfolio volatility mean annual returns cannot fall below −13.17%?","No. Standard deviation is a dispersion parameter, not an absolute bound; actual tails, correlations and forced cash flows can produce larger losses."]]},{"title":"Correlation regimes, stress testing and changing risk estimates","body":["Historical covariance matrices are sensitive to the return sample, asset proxies, frequency, look-back horizon and market regime. Equity and government bond correlation can be negative during some growth scares but positive during inflation shocks when both equity valuations and bond prices fall. Currency hedges, credit duration and leverage can change relationships further. Portfolio construction should ask what economic force makes each asset rise or fall rather than assume permanent risk cancellation.","Create a scenario table in addition to a covariance table. Scenario A is ordinary growth with stocks +8% and bonds +3%; Scenario B is recession with stocks −35% and bonds +5%; Scenario C is inflation shock with stocks −25% and bonds −12%. A 60/40 portfolio in these three independent illustrations returns +6%, −19% and −19.8% respectively, before costs and taxes. A simple volatility calculation from prior quiet years can miss simultaneous price declines.","Distinguish scenario modeling from probability-weighted forecasts. The above shock scenarios are plausible examples, not observations from a live 2026 market or assigned probabilities. A stress matrix can include unemployment, a home price decline, liquidity spread widening and rising borrowing costs together. Since stress events can correlate across a person's salary, car, mortgage collateral and investments, the household balance sheet is the true object of risk management.","Model uncertainty should be displayed explicitly. A decision based on one estimated correlation .25 should be tested at −.25, +.25 and +.75, and under combinations of higher asset volatilities. This sensitivity is especially valuable when an optimizer recommends unrealistically high leverage or a narrow asset with an apparently low historical volatility. A robust allocation survives a range of uncertain parameters."],"worked":["60/40 portfolio, stock +8%, bond +3% ⇒ .6×8+.4×3=6%.","Recession stock −35%, bond +5% ⇒ −21%+2%=−19%.","Inflation shock stock −25%, bond −12% ⇒ −15%−4.8%=−19.8%.","Simultaneous losses can exceed the investor's capacity even when historical average correlation appeared favorable.","Scenarios require both asset-return and household cash-flow considerations."],"practice":[["Why can stocks and bonds decline together during a particular economic shock?","Inflation and required-yield repricing can reduce equity valuations and fixed-income prices simultaneously, making correlation regime-dependent."],["What is the role of an adverse scenario relative to a historical covariance estimate?","It tests economic conditions and joint losses that historical sampling or smooth normal models may underrepresent, especially when a liability date cannot change."]]},{"title":"Risk concentration, employer exposure and factor look-through","body":["Portfolio risk includes more than assets listed at a broker. A worker whose compensation and job security depend on a technology employer already bears economic exposure to that industry. Owning employer stock or a narrow technology index may concentrate salary risk and financial assets in the same downturn. A family owning a leveraged house in one Connecticut town may also be exposed to local property values, labor conditions and insurance costs, even if the home is not a line item in the securities portfolio.","Look-through concentration calculates holdings across overlapping funds. Suppose fund A and fund B each represent half the account, and the same issuer group represents 40% of A and 60% of B. Combined exposure is .5×.4+.5×.6=.5, or 50% of the account. Counting the two fund wrappers as two diversified positions conceals a large shared factor. Sector and country weights can similarly overlap even when fund indexes have different names.","Concentration may amplify loss. In a $20,000 portfolio, a single stock at 35% weight contributes $7,000 exposure. If that stock loses 60% and all other holdings are flat, portfolio loss is $4,200 or 21%. This ignores spillovers to other assets and employment that can worsen the outcome. A formal investment policy may impose maximum issuer, employer stock and sector allocations and require a defined response when drift exceeds these limits.","Some diversifiers can be illiquid or expensive. Alternative investments may report smoother appraised values than publicly traded securities and can still share underlying leverage or property market risk. A low reported quarterly correlation does not create cash to pay a sudden closing bill. Portfolio policy must balance correlation improvement with legal redemption restrictions and total economic fees."],"worked":["Portfolio $20,000 and single issuer 35% ⇒ exposure $7,000.","Issuer suffers −60% while others flat ⇒ loss $4,200, portfolio −21%.","Two equal funds with 40% and 60% common issuer-group exposure ⇒ combined 50% group weight.","Employer income and home collateral can amplify the same regional or industry downturn.","Fund count and low reported correlation are not substitutes for look-through ownership analysis."],"practice":[["What hidden risk arises when a worker invests heavily in shares of the same employer who pays their wages?","A company-specific or industry shock can affect both financial investments and future income, creating concentrated total household risk."],["How can two diversified-looking funds leave half the total account in one issuer group?","Overlapping underlying holdings compound exposure; the common-group weight is the weighted sum of each fund's holdings, not determined by wrapper count."]]},{"title":"Sharpe ratio, VaR, expected shortfall and what they omit","body":["Risk-adjusted performance statistics can support comparisons only when definitions and data are consistent. The Sharpe ratio is (portfolio expected or average excess return relative to a defined risk-free rate)/portfolio volatility over the matching horizon. A portfolio with modeled annual return 8%, cash proxy 2% and standard deviation 15% has Sharpe (8−2)/15=.4. This is not a return, a probability of loss or a contractual performance guarantee. Negative ratios and short noisy records require especially careful interpretation.","Value at Risk (VaR) at a specified confidence and horizon estimates a loss threshold exceeded with some probability under the model, not a maximum possible loss. For a one-year normal-return toy model with expected return 5%, standard deviation 15% and 5% lower-tail z≈−1.645, lower return quantile is 5%−1.645×15%≈−19.675%. On $20,000 that approximates a $3,935 loss at the 5th percentile, under extremely strong normal-distribution assumptions. Losses beyond that threshold remain possible.","Expected shortfall or conditional VaR examines average loss beyond a chosen tail threshold, making it often more informative about extreme downside. A simple historical maximum drawdown measures peak-to-trough decline across an observed path, not the next period's worst-case loss. All such measures are vulnerable to stale price samples, regime changes, autocorrelation and hidden leverage. Model outputs should be cross-checked with scenarios that do not rely on normal distributions.","Be precise about the investor's question. Annual VaR might be relevant for risk capital but is a poor direct measure of whether a $12,000 home closing can be financed next Tuesday. The timing and minimum required dollars are more actionable than one aggregate statistical parameter. A good portfolio analysis states horizon, confidence, input sources and a stop-or-replenish rule when household obligations are threatened."],"worked":["Modeled annual portfolio return 8%, comparison cash proxy 2%, volatility 15%.","Sharpe ratio (8−2)/15=.40 under matching units.","Normal toy lower 5% quantile=5%−1.645×15%≈−19.675%.","On $20,000, modeled 5% tail threshold loss ≈$3,935.","A 95% VaR does not cap the worst possible loss at $3,935."],"practice":[["Why does a 95% Value at Risk number not mean a portfolio cannot lose more than the displayed amount?","By definition the threshold can be exceeded in the remaining tail, and model assumptions can fail; VaR is not a worst-case bound."],["How can two portfolios with similar annual volatility carry different household risks?","Liquidity, concentrated exposures, return tails, correlation with wages, tax and withdrawal timing can differ even when standard deviations are similar."]]},{"title":"Quantitative portfolio committee: validate rather than worship an optimizer","body":["A modern optimizer can solve for portfolio weights based on expected returns, covariances and constraints, but mathematically optimal weights can be economically fragile when inputs are estimated inaccurately. Mean-variance optimization often amplifies small forecast differences into corner portfolios with extreme asset allocations or short positions. Constraining individual positions, leverage and turnover and testing multiple market regimes can improve robustness, but every numerical output remains conditional.","An academic recommendation should give a clear covariance matrix, the date and method used to estimate it, the proposed asset weights, and a stress table including both recession and inflation shocks. The student should demonstrate positive semidefinite covariance when modeling more than two assets and ensure weights sum to the stated total, avoiding impossible negative variances. It should also compare gross expected return with expected return after fees and taxes, while acknowledging that fee savings do not remove market risk.","Take a 60/40 model with stock σ20%, bond σ8%, correlation .25 and expected returns 7%/3%. The simple portfolio expected return is .6×7%+.4×3%=5.4%; portfolio standard deviation from the earlier variance formula is approximately 13.17%. An optimizer might recommend a different mix after modestly changing bond expected return or correlation. The committee must ask whether such sensitivity is justified by real evidence or is an artifact of input estimation noise.","Close with an explicit suitability decision. An equity-heavy portfolio may be tolerable for long-term retirement surplus but not for a down-payment reserve. The student should provide evidence that accessible cash remains funded after a combined market, job and emergency-spending shock. In education and professional practice, realistic constraints and audited arithmetic are more valuable than an impressive chart with invented precision."],"worked":["Expected stock 7%, bond 3%, weights 60/40 ⇒ expected return 5.4%.","Portfolio volatility for σ20%/8%, ρ=.25 is approximately 13.1697%.","Adverse shock stock −35%, bond +5% ⇒ portfolio −19%.","Separate due-date cash reserve must remain outside risk optimization unless loss capacity is documented.","A sensitivity matrix should test whether recommended allocations move drastically under small input revisions."],"practice":[["Why may a mean-variance optimizer produce unstable or concentrated allocations?","Estimated expected returns and covariances contain error, and optimization can magnify tiny differences unless robust constraints and economic judgment are applied."],["What should the final risk memorandum contain beyond an expected-return and volatility chart?","Liquidity obligations, actual holdings, parameter sources, concentration, tax and cost assumptions, adverse scenarios and explicit implementation and review limits."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFA 2026 Portfolio Management Curriculum","https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/basics-of-portfolio-planning-and-construction"],["Investor.gov Asset Allocation Diversification","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"],["CFA Portfolio Risk and Return learning materials","https://www.cfainstitute.org/programs/cfa-program/candidate-resources/level-i-exam"],["Investor.gov Index Funds and ETF Risks","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/mutual-funds-and-exchange-traded-funds"],["Investor.gov Understanding Risk","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-risk"],["FINRA Diversification and Asset Allocation","https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investing-basics/asset-allocation-diversification"]],"assessment":{"title":"Portfolio covariance, correlated household loss and risk-budget committee","prompt":"Calculate covariance for stock σ20%, bond σ8% and correlation .25, then 60/40 expected return and standard deviation. Recompute volatility for ρ=−.25, +.25 and +.75. Stress stock/bond outcomes of +8/+3, −35/+5, −25/−12. Quantify issuer concentration at 35% of $20,000 with −60% security loss, Sharpe (8−2)/15 and normal VaR with expected 5%, σ15% and z=−1.645. Explain limits of VaR and optimization, and portfolio suitability for a dated $12,000 home purchase.","rubric":["Covariance and return definition (20%)","Portfolio variance with correlation sensitivity (25%)","Market and household downside stress (25%)","Risk statistics and model limitations (20%)","Reproducible primary sources and decision rules (10%)"]}},{"id":"L10.S3","title":"Rebalancing and Costs","subtitle":"Portfolio drift, trade sizing, cash-flow corrections, taxes, fees and execution discipline","prerequisites":["L10.S1 strategic target allocation and buckets","L10.S2 portfolio weights and risk concentration","L6.S1 tax basis and capital gains"],"objectives":["Compute current allocation drift and target dollar holdings","Calculate full rebalancing trades without confusing percentage points","Use contributions before selling when appropriate","Compare threshold and calendar triggers without promising returns","Analyze taxable-gain realization and tax-lot evidence","Estimate turnover, fund operating costs and bid-ask spreads","Document trade execution, wash sale and account restriction checks"],"sections":[{"title":"Allocation drift turns successful securities into new concentrations","body":["Portfolio weights move when component prices earn different returns or when deposits and withdrawals are uneven. A stock/bond mix initially chosen for suitable risk can become stock-heavy after a sustained equity rally, leaving the owner exposed to larger drawdowns just when a future expense is approaching. Rebalancing returns actual holdings toward policy target percentages; it is risk-control and implementation, not a demonstrated method for reliably raising returns in every market regime.","Consider a starting $10,000 portfolio with a 60% stock and 40% bond target. Later stock market value is $8,000 and bonds are $4,000, so portfolio value is $12,000. Stock weight is 8,000/12,000=66.6667%, a 6.6667-percentage-point drift above the 60% target. The target stock value on current capital is .60×$12,000=$7,200, so a full rebalance sells $800 of stocks and uses proceeds to buy $800 in bonds, before taxes, fees and price changes.","The difference between percentage change and percentage-point drift matters. The stock share moved from 60% to 66.67%, a 6.67-point increase, or about 11.11% relative increase in stock portfolio weight. A policy with a 5-percentage-point absolute drift trigger would be activated by a 66.67% stock share; a policy based on relative 5% of target uses a different boundary. The terms must be written precisely before looking at market results.","Real accounts may include international holdings, cash and restricted securities. The sum of every asset-class target must equal 100% unless the policy intentionally models leverage or shorts. Corporate actions and unsettled trades can change weights. Use same-day mark-to-market values and consistent tax treatment so a seemingly balanced screenshot is not an arithmetic artifact."],"worked":["Later stocks $8,000; bonds $4,000 ⇒ portfolio $12,000.","Stock current weight $8,000/$12,000=66.6667%.","60% stock target ×$12,000=$7,200; target bonds $4,800.","Trade $800 stock sale and $800 bond purchase to restore 60/40 before costs.","Absolute drift 6.6667 percentage points from the 60% stock target."],"practice":[["Why does an 800-dollar stock sale restore a 60/40 allocation in the example?","After the sale stocks equal $7,200, bonds receive $800 and equal $4,800, giving precisely 60% and 40% of the unchanged $12,000 total before frictions."],["Why should a rebalancing rule specify whether its trigger uses percentage points or relative percent drift?","They produce different action thresholds, so ambiguous wording can cause inconsistent and costly trades when market weights move."]]},{"title":"Rebalance with incoming contributions before selling appreciated positions","body":["New cash deposits can shift portfolio weights without forcing existing gains to be realized. When one asset category is overweight and another underweight, directing contributions to the underweight category may gradually restore the planned exposure while preserving flexibility. The method is not always fast enough to meet a large drift threshold, and it can leave risk elevated during the transition. A disciplined policy should specify when contribution-only correction is permitted and when selling becomes necessary.","Return to the $8,000 stocks/$4,000 bonds case targeting 60/40. If the investor adds $1,333.33 of entirely new cash to bonds, portfolio value becomes $13,333.33 and stocks become approximately 60% of total, while bond value becomes $5,333.33. The required cash can be derived by solving $8,000/(12,000+x)=.60, so x=8,000/.60−12,000≈$1,333.33. That is much more than an $800 reallocation trade, because adding only to bonds increases the portfolio denominator rather than moving existing dollars.","If the household contributes only $250 each month, it would require more than five unchanged months to accumulate $1,333.33 for this specific contribution-only correction, assuming no price change. A market move can alter the needed contribution before completion. The investor should not treat future contributions as current cash or assume wages remain uninterrupted during economic stress. When policy limits are breached, the trade-off is current concentration versus avoiding taxable sales.","Compare contribution-only rebalancing with using dividends and interest distributions, tax-advantaged account trades, or selective sales of appreciated positions. Asset-location and tax-lot choices matter, but investment constraints should remain primary. Rebalancing a retirement account may have different current tax effects than realizing a gain in a taxable brokerage account; verify the account before executing."],"worked":["Stocks $8,000, bonds $4,000, target stock share 60%.","Contribution x to bonds solves $8,000/($12,000+x)=.60.","Required new bond purchase from external cash x≈$1,333.33.","At $250 per month, at least six contributions are needed if no markets move.","A sale of $800 stock reallocates faster but may have tax and transaction implications."],"practice":[["Why does adding $800 to bonds not completely correct the stock weight in the example?","Total capital becomes $12,800 and stocks remain $8,000 or 62.5%; contribution-only correction requires about $1,333.33 to reach 60%."],["What is the main trade-off when waiting for future contributions to correct drift?","It can reduce immediate realized taxes and trading but leaves the portfolio temporarily exposed to weights beyond its preferred target."]]},{"title":"Rebalance on a calendar, a threshold or a life event","body":["A calendar rule might review allocation each six or twelve months regardless of whether drift is small. A threshold rule triggers a review after an absolute or relative deviation from targets becomes sufficiently large. A combination rule uses scheduled reviews with exceptions for severe drift or changed obligations. The SEC notes that rebalancing can be done by selling overweight assets, directing new purchases toward underweight assets or altering ongoing deposits. It also warns that fees and taxes matter and that frequent rebalancing is generally not necessary.","Consider a target 60% stocks with allowable band ±5 percentage points. Actual weight at 64% is inside the 55%–65% band; 66.7% is outside and triggers an action review. The trigger should not automatically force a market order without first checking liquidity, taxes, trading restrictions and actual cash needs. The investor might rebalance to exact 60% or to a nearer allowable boundary under a documented method, and those choices produce different turnover.","A life event can outweigh the calendar. A planned home purchase advances from three years to six months, or a worker loses a job and needs cash. Those facts change permissible risk capacity and can justify updating strategic allocation rather than merely restoring an outdated target. Conversely, a news headline predicting recession is not itself a personal-liability change. A clear policy distinguishes external changes in objectives from attempts to predict daily price action.","An analytical comparison of annual versus drift-trigger rules should include all observed dates, executed trades, taxes, costs and whether risk constraints were met. Backtests are especially sensitive to which asset returns and price histories were used. An author who chooses thresholds after observing history risks overfitting; define candidate strategies before measuring their results."],"worked":["Stock target 60% with ±5 percentage-point policy band ⇒ acceptable 55% through 65%.","Observed 64% remains inside the band; observed 66.7% breaches it.","At current portfolio $12,000, moving to 60% requires $800 sale as modeled.","Moving only to 65% would require stock holdings $7,800, an $200 sale before frictions.","Changing home timing requires review of target policy itself, not merely mechanical rebalancing."],"practice":[["Why is a six-month scheduled review not a commitment to buy or sell securities every six months?","Review identifies actual drift, life changes and constraints; a suitable portfolio may remain within policy ranges without any trade."],["Why should investors avoid backtesting dozens of threshold rules and selecting only the historically best one?","Choosing a rule after seeing outcomes can overfit noise and overstate future performance, particularly after fees, tax and changing market conditions."]]},{"title":"Capital-gain realization, tax lots and after-tax trade decisions","body":["Rebalancing a taxable brokerage account can realize gains or losses when a security is sold. The economic gain for a specifically identified tax lot generally depends on proceeds, properly adjusted tax basis and applicable costs. Holding period influences whether tax treatment is short or long term under federal rules; Connecticut income tax and other federal provisions may also matter. A gross trade that looks sensible before taxes can be costly if it realizes substantial appreciated shares unnecessarily.","Suppose a taxpayer sells $800 of stock from an eligible identified lot whose allocable adjusted cost basis is $500. Gross realized gain is $300 before selling costs. At a fictional flat 15% federal long-term capital-gains rate, conditional federal tax on that gain would be $45. That is an example, not an assertion that this particular person qualifies for 15% or has no other tax. Another lot with adjusted basis $750 may result in only $50 gross realized gain, assuming basis and lot identification are lawful and supported.","Loss harvesting can offset gains subject to statutory rules and carryovers, but a wash sale may disallow or defer the deduction when substantially identical securities are acquired within the relevant period. Tax treatment of transactions involving spouses and IRA arrangements can also complicate the outcome; confirm with current IRS instructions. Selling solely to create a paper loss without considering economic exposure, spread and the replacement instrument can fail either the risk or tax objective.","Implement tax-aware rebalancing by checking account type, current unrealized gain or loss by lot, holding period, dividend reinvestment activity, buy/sell settlement and trading costs. Never fabricate basis or assume all index ETFs are legally non-identical for wash-sale purposes merely because tickers differ. The operational goal is maintaining portfolio risk while avoiding preventable taxes and respecting the evidence on the actual statement."],"worked":["Sale proceeds $800, allocable basis $500 ⇒ hypothetical gain $300.","Illustrative eligible long-term tax rate 15% ⇒ conditional federal tax $45.","Another supported lot with basis $750 would produce $50 gain under same $800 proceeds.","Trading fees and applicable state taxes could change net amount.","Use actual lot records, tax classification and wash-sale rules rather than defaults."],"practice":[["Why should a taxable rebalancing calculation include specific acquisition lots rather than simply multiplying the sale by a flat tax rate?","Only the recognized taxable gain or loss, computed using lawful adjusted basis and holding period, drives gains treatment, not all sale proceeds."],["Why can immediately repurchasing a substantially identical security after harvesting a loss defeat an expected tax benefit?","Wash-sale rules can disallow or defer certain loss deductions, so the transaction timing and replacement security must be documented."]]},{"title":"Transaction frictions: expense ratios, spreads, turnover and advisory fees","body":["Funds and advisory arrangements can contain ongoing expense ratios, management fees, trading bid-ask spreads and sometimes account or commission charges. A fund expense ratio reduces investment performance through recurring fund operations, while bid-ask spread and commissions affect execution when trading. Taxable capital-gain realization adds another cost category. Investor disclosures often present some of these costs differently, so adding identical fees from a net performance number can mistakenly double count them.","Suppose a hypothetical portfolio earns 6% annual gross before a 0.25% fund operating cost and a 0.75% advisory management fee, both approximated as a simple 1% combined annual drag. Starting $40,000 over twenty years at smooth illustrative 6% gross would become $40,000×1.06^20, while a rough 5% net return would become $40,000×1.05^20. The future-value difference is substantial, and the values are simplified models rather than actual market forecasts or a precise daily fee accounting simulation.","Rebalancing trades can involve spreads. If the portfolio sells $800 of an ETF and buys $800 of a different ETF with a 0.20% effective total round-trip execution burden under an illustrative assumption, direct trading cost is about $1.60, distinct from any taxes. Real spreads vary by security, trade size, volatility and the market session; a higher-cost illiquid instrument may impose a much greater impact than that toy number.","A risk policy should state acceptable total ongoing fees, turnover frequency, security liquidity thresholds and how actual after-tax returns are reported. Low cost improves expected net wealth conditional on comparable exposure but does not make volatile securities safe for dated obligations. A good rebalancing process restores a risk target at reasonable cost rather than treating higher trading activity as evidence of professional attention."],"worked":["Portfolio $40,000, hypothetical gross rate 6%, approximate annual combined fees 1% ⇒ net assumed 5%.","Twenty-year gross value $40,000×1.06^20; simple net value $40,000×1.05^20.","First-year 1% simplified fee on $40,000 corresponds to $400 at unchanged base.","Executed trade notional $800 with hypothetical 0.20% execution cost ⇒ $1.60 cost.","Operating fees, capital-gain tax and trading spreads are distinct categories."],"practice":[["Why is frequent rebalancing not automatically beneficial even if it always restores target percentages?","Each trade can generate spreads, fees and taxable gains, while market movement can cause immediate new drift; the process must justify risk benefit relative to costs."],["What is the difference between a recurring fund expense ratio and an ETF bid-ask spread?","Ongoing fund expenses are reflected through fund performance over time, while the bid-ask spread is a trading-price friction incurred when buying or selling."]]},{"title":"Rebalancing under stress, with withdrawal and tax constraints","body":["Investment policy rules must operate when markets and household cash flow behave badly. Rebalancing an underweight stock position during a severe drawdown can be appropriate for a long-horizon investor with intact cash reserves, but reckless when unemployment has created immediate spending needs. A mechanical system should be subordinate to a liquidity floor and liability deadlines. The investor should not sell protected home funds or use margin borrowing simply to reach a target percentage.","Suppose a $20,000 retirement portfolio begins at 60/40: $12,000 stocks and $8,000 bonds. Stocks fall 30%, bonds rise 5%, resulting in $8,400 stocks and $8,400 bonds, or 50/50 on $16,800. Returning to 60/40 would require target stock $10,080 and bonds $6,720, so the investor buys $1,680 stocks using bond sales, before fees. That may be suitable if the long-horizon policy and outside cash reserves remain valid, but it meaningfully changes near-term exposures.","Now impose an income shock and a need to withdraw $3,000 from the same account within a month. The investment committee should first fund the legally and financially required withdrawal, quantify after-tax costs, and then recalculate the target based on remaining assets and revised risk capacity. Selling $1,680 bonds to buy stocks while separately borrowing $3,000 at expensive credit-card rates could be an economically incoherent sequence. Risk capacity has changed, so restoring a stale target is not necessarily appropriate.","A robust plan records cash needs, allowable tax lots, rebalancing thresholds, maximum transaction expenses, no-margin rule and the authority to suspend trading when obligations cannot be covered. Consider how orders execute in illiquid conditions and whether settlement dates allow payments. Analytical sophistication is judged by maintaining a workable household plan through shocks, not by automatic adherence to a formula."],"worked":["Original stocks $12,000, bonds $8,000 in $20,000 retirement account.","Stock shock −30% ⇒ $8,400; bond return +5% ⇒ $8,400; total $16,800.","Target stock 60%×$16,800=$10,080; bond target $6,720.","Full rebalance buys $1,680 stocks funded by $1,680 bond sales before fees.","A near-term withdrawal requirement should be considered before trading to meet an old target."],"practice":[["Why might a 1,680-dollar stock purchase be correct under one household scenario and wrong under another?","The strategic mix may remain appropriate for well-funded long-horizon capital, but new income loss or near-term withdrawals can reduce the household's financial ability to take that risk."],["What is the first step before rebalancing when an investor must withdraw 3,000 dollars for a required bill?","Reassess accessible cash, taxes, settlement and whether the original allocation and risk capacity still apply after the necessary withdrawal."]]},{"title":"Operational rebalancing memorandum and decision audit","body":["The final rebalancing deliverable should include market values by asset class, weights and targets, permissible bands, the dated reason for action and trade calculations. It should identify whether a contribution-only solution can restore weights, or whether selling existing positions is necessary. Taxable accounts require a lot-level gains/losses schedule, while tax-advantaged accounts require confirmation of permitted account transactions and withdrawal restrictions. Every cost assumption belongs in the record.","Analyze three candidate plans for the $8,000 stock/$4,000 bond portfolio: no trade while weights remain 66.7/33.3, full rebalance with $800 stock sale/bond purchase, or contribution-only correction with approximately $1,333 of fresh money. Compare post-action weights, taxes, cost, need for cash and continued risk exposure. A single answer cannot be correct without specifying whether the household has free cash, a tax-sensitive account and an absolute 5-point drift policy.","Include a test against policy breaches. If a single issuer grows above the maximum 10% holding limit or total equities pass an allowed 65% ceiling, how quickly can the exposure be reduced without violating an account restriction or a home cash floor? A committee might sell only enough to return inside the band, or fully reset, but it must document the method. It should not change the policy threshold after observing which trade would have worked best.","The academically useful product is a reusable decision log, not merely a correct number. The log captures date, assets, trades, realized gains, total expenses, risk before/after and expected evidence for the next review. It provides continuity through household changes and prevents a market rally or downturn from rewriting the family's objectives."],"worked":["Existing total $12,000; stocks $8,000/12,000=66.667%.","Option A no trade retains risk above a 65% maximum under a fictional policy.","Option B $800 stock sale plus $800 bond purchase fully restores 60/40.","Option C new $1,333.33 entirely to bonds approaches 60/40 without an existing sale.","After-tax costs and availability of new cash determine which feasible plan is superior."],"practice":[["Why should an investment committee keep a dated record of each rebalancing decision?","It shows whether the trade followed predefined policy and real household constraints, and permits later evaluation without hindsight or selective rewriting."],["Why can a contribution-only plan fail to be feasible even though its algebra reaches the target weights?","The required external cash may exceed affordable savings, arrive too late or compete with near-term liabilities, so mathematical feasibility is not household feasibility."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["Investor.gov Allocation, Diversification and Rebalancing","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"],["Investor.gov Beginners Guide to Allocation","https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/general-resources/publications-research/info-sheets/beginners-guide-asset"],["IRS Publication 550 Investment Income and Expenses","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"],["IRS Publication 551 Basis of Assets","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p551"],["Investor.gov Fund Expenses","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/mutual-funds-and-exchange-traded-funds/mutual-fund-fees-and-expenses"],["CFA 2026 Basics of Portfolio Planning","https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/basics-of-portfolio-planning-and-construction"]],"assessment":{"title":"Tax-aware rebalancing and risk capacity board case","prompt":"Given stocks $8,000 and bonds $4,000 after an original 60/40 allocation, compute weights and a full-trade rebalance, plus the cash contribution required to restore stock target without selling. Compare 5 percentage-point drift trigger, taxable sale proceeds $800 with basis $500, illustrative 15% eligible tax, and a separate fund fee scenario. Then stress $20,000 retirement account stocks −30%, bonds +5%, calculate 60/40 rebalancing trades and assess how an immediate $3,000 expense changes suitability.","rubric":["Weights and exact trade sizing (25%)","Contributions and policy threshold design (20%)","Tax, costs and account restrictions (25%)","Stress-case revised risk capacity (20%)","Auditable decision log and sources (10%)"]}},{"id":"L10.S4","title":"Building an Investment Policy","subtitle":"Institutional-grade personal IPS, constraints, governance, performance and decision rules","prerequisites":["L10.S1 household funding buckets and risk capacity","L10.S2 covariance, stress and concentration","L10.S3 rebalancing and taxable costs"],"objectives":["Draft a complete investment policy statement with measurable goals","Specify return and risk objectives separately","Document liquidity, horizon, legal, tax and unique constraints","Define strategic allocation with allowed ranges and account roles","Create review, governance, exception and rebalancing protocols","Evaluate after-tax, risk-adjusted performance against appropriate benchmarks","Integrate home purchase, retirement and beneficiary succession"],"sections":[{"title":"What a real investment policy statement does for decision quality","body":["An Investment Policy Statement (IPS) documents investment purpose, financial circumstances, objectives, constraints, implementation and governance before the next market shock. CFA Institute guidance identifies client circumstances and constraints as foundations for suitability, including liquidity, horizon, tax, legal requirements and unique conditions. In personal finance, the IPS also provides a coordination document for partners, a trustee or financial professional who may need to understand what assets are meant to accomplish.","An IPS is not an inflexible promise to hold every investment forever. It gives the investor clear procedures to change policy when circumstances change and distinguishes such revisions from panic trading. A document that states only 'buy stocks and keep them long-term' is insufficient because it lacks near-term cash obligations, financial loss capacity, authorized actions, fees and criteria for success. A policy can rationally choose cash for a short dated goal and a diversified mix for retirement.","Write a precise financial purpose: protect a $6,000 emergency reserve continuously; preserve $12,000 home-closing resources due within twelve months; and contribute a realistically sustainable amount to long-term wealth without leaving essential bills unpaid. If the family cannot currently fund all three objectives, the policy should state the shortfall and a priority order rather than use one investment dollar to satisfy several targets simultaneously.","Governance includes document owner, revision approval, review dates and record storage. The IPS should name any household beneficiary and the conditions under which a trustee or surviving partner can access assets. Such details are not substitutes for executed legal trusts, beneficiary designations or estate counsel, but they help prevent investment arrangements from conflicting with actual family commitments."],"worked":["IPS goal: emergency reserve $6,000 immediately accessible.","Separate home goal $12,000 due in twelve months.","If household accessible cash is $18,000, entire amount is assigned to the two protected goals.","New long-term contributions must be measured against positive actual monthly household surplus.","Policy owner and authorized revisions should be named with dated signatures."],"practice":[["Why is a one-line statement to 'invest aggressively for retirement' not an adequate IPS?","It omits cash needs, legal limitations, risk capacity, target exposure, cost and tax controls, rebalancing and review rules necessary to judge decisions."],["How does an IPS improve household decisions during market volatility?","It documents agreed goals and decision triggers beforehand, making responses to changing prices less dependent on fear, excitement or persuasive promotion."]]},{"title":"Objectives: distinguish required return, acceptable loss and shortfall probability","body":["An IPS should define return objectives using real dates and amounts, including taxes, fees, inflation and contributions. A projected return assumption is not a contractual rate. Calculate the required contribution for a goal and test whether achievable income and savings can support it before choosing the risk category. A household target may include maintaining purchasing power, funding a retirement spending stream and leaving a bequest with clear beneficiary access.","Risk objectives should describe financial loss capacity and behavioral tolerance, not rely solely on an abstract standard deviation. Identify worst acceptable drawdown for each bucket, shortfall probability or loss threshold under specified scenarios, minimum cash reserve and a rule for cutting discretionary investment when income falls. A risk limit such as 'no more than 10% single issuer exposure' is operationally actionable; 'avoid risk' is not.","Consider a goal to fund $20,000 in 24 months from $12,000 now. With no investment growth, funding requires an additional $8,000 over 24 payments, or about $333.33 monthly. If the household can save only $250, the model projects a $2,000 shortfall. A portfolio that advertises 15% expected return does not eliminate this risk; high expected returns generally introduce uncertainty that may be inappropriate for the closing date.","Classify goals as mandatory, important but adjustable, and discretionary. Mortgage payment and essential medical spending are not speculative return objectives. A vacation or optional purchase has more flexibility. The IPS should prioritize funded ratios, income coverage and dates over the psychologically appealing headline account balance."],"worked":["Current goal savings $12,000; dated target $20,000 in 24 months.","Zero-growth required saving ($20,000−$12,000)/24≈$333.33 monthly.","If only $250 is affordable, contributions total $6,000 and expected principal $18,000 before earnings.","The base-case cash gap is $2,000 under these assumptions.","Risk tolerance language should be supplemented by concrete shortfall and drawdown limits."],"practice":[["Why must a return objective specify whether it is a required result or an assumption used in a forecast?","The market does not guarantee an expected result, and a mathematically required but unrealistic return signals an underfunded objective rather than a reliable strategy."],["Why should an IPS distinguish mandatory spending from discretionary goals?","Necessary bills and contractual commitments have less timing flexibility and therefore lower capacity for risky or illiquid investment than deferrable consumption."]]},{"title":"Constraints: liquidity, horizon, tax, legal and unique circumstances","body":["The five common major investment constraints are liquidity requirements, time horizon, taxes, legal and regulatory circumstances, and unique individual needs. These categories interact: an illiquid private fund may be acceptable in a pension trust with decades of investment horizon but unsuitable for a family's home-closing money. A tax-advantaged account may improve long-run after-tax returns while restricting access for short-horizon cash. A donor or trustee may have explicit legal limitations different from the original investor's preferences.","Liquidity constraints should quantify an accessible dollar floor and identify settlement or transfer deadlines. The time horizon can contain several goals, not one age-based label. Tax constraints include account registration, current income and capital-gain treatment, basis records and state taxation. Legal constraints can include creditor liens, pension account rules, trust investment duties, employer stock restrictions and beneficiary rights. Unique circumstances include disability needs, a dependent child, ethical restrictions or a household member's ability to understand and manage financial records.","Use a fictional Connecticut household preparing to purchase a $250,000 home with a potential $237,500 mortgage. A planned inheritance for a daughter creates future cash and ownership questions. The IPS must not promise the house is freely transferable merely because it will be held in a trust; mortgage covenants, recorded assistance liens and legal title require separate review. The household's accessible assets should not be counted as investment collateral and closing liquidity at the same time.","Document each constraint with proof: bank statements, signed debt contracts, insurance declarations, court or trustee instruments where relevant, employer blackout windows and tax account terms. Financial experts should be consulted for decisions requiring legal or tax judgments. The IPS captures assumptions and responsibilities; it does not by itself grant authority that the legal contracts lack."],"worked":["Liquidity floor $6,000 emergency plus $12,000 home closing=$18,000.","Nominal $250,000 house less $237,500 illustrative mortgage ⇒ $12,500 gross equity before costs.","Bank assets used for closing cannot be double counted as unrestricted retirement risk capital.","Tax-deferred retirement account access may differ from bank cash access.","Executed liens and trust documents control legal transfer and beneficiary rights."],"practice":[["Why may an investment in a tax-deferred retirement account be unsuitable for a required home-closing cash reserve?","Withdrawal eligibility, tax and penalty rules, timing and market exposures may prevent safe or inexpensive access when the closing payment is due."],["Why must an IPS list trust and mortgage restrictions separately from personal goals?","Investment intent does not override lender contracts or trustees' legal duties, and beneficiaries' rights may not match the investor's unrestricted wishes."]]},{"title":"Strategic asset mix, limits and explicit rebalancing governance","body":["The asset allocation section should specify which holdings belong to each goal and the permitted range of stocks, bonds, cash and alternatives, with maximum issuer and sector exposures. Its target should be realistic for available assets, fees and account access. The same target percentage can be implemented using different securities with different credit, duration, tax and fund costs; the IPS should identify allowable categories and prohibitions without requiring one specific ticker.","An illustrative long-term retirement pool of $40,000 might target 60% broad equities, 30% high-quality fixed income and 10% liquidity, yielding $24,000, $12,000 and $4,000. A policy band could permit equities 55%–65%, subject to a separate maximum 10% individual issuer limit and prohibition on margin borrowing. Those constraints are examples, not a universal recommendation. A long-run investor with ample pension benefits and different spending might rationally choose other weights.","The rebalancing rule should state check frequency, trigger, trade sequencing and costs. For example: review every six months, correct a target deviation exceeding 5 percentage points or a liquidity floor breach, use new contributions and distributions first, and verify taxes and account restrictions before sales. Specify whether correction returns exactly to strategic targets or only within allowed bands. A trigger to review is not permission to place an order that would violate a cash withdrawal deadline.","Exception governance matters. If a job loss reduces savings capacity, the policy may temporarily pause long-term contributions and replenish emergency cash. A trustee or financial advisor should know who may revise targets, where revisions are stored, and whether a change requires agreement from both household decision-makers. Every exception should cite a changed financial fact, not just a market forecast."],"worked":["Long-run investment pool $40,000, target stocks 60%=$24,000.","Bonds 30%=$12,000; cash 10%=$4,000.","Equity allowed policy band example 55%–65%; issuer maximum 10%.","At 66.7% equities, a 5-point absolute drift limit from 60% is breached.","Rebalancing execution must respect taxes and the separate near-term liquidity floor."],"practice":[["What is the difference between a target allocation and a permitted band?","The target expresses the desired strategic weight; the band specifies a defined range before a review or corrective action is triggered."],["Why should a rebalancing policy specify use of new contributions before taxable sales when feasible?","It can reduce unnecessary realized gain and trading costs while still correcting drift, though sometimes slower than immediately reallocating current holdings."]]},{"title":"Benchmarks, risk-adjusted performance and honest review","body":["An IPS performance section should compare results with a benchmark reflecting the actual strategic allocation and cash-flow dates, not merely a popular equity index. A retirement portfolio at 60/30/10 cannot fairly be described as failed because it underperformed a 100% stock index during a stock bull market without considering its lower downside risk and different liquidity aims. Conversely, a concentrated fund's strong short-term return does not prove its risk and fee budget was appropriate.","Performance must distinguish time-weighted return, which generally neutralizes the effect of external contributions and withdrawals for manager evaluation, from money-weighted internal rate of return, which reflects the household's actual dated investment cash flows. A person depositing heavily just before a market drawdown can have poor money-weighted experience even when the fund's long-period reported time-weighted return is positive. A portfolio review should show net-of-fee outcomes and tax results when relevant.","Assume a fictional account begins at $10,000, rises to $11,000, then receives a $9,000 contribution and finishes at $18,000. Before the contribution the account earned 10%; after the contribution its value fell from $20,000 to $18,000, a −10% period return. Linked time-weighted growth factor is 1.10×.90=.99, hence time-weighted return −1%. The investor's actual dollar loss and money-weighted return depend on exact contribution date and time horizon, so an evaluator must not confuse the two.","Annual review should compare goal funded ratios, drift, asset exposure, fees, taxable realizations, emergency cash and downside scenarios. When the portfolio grows but home savings fall behind because housing costs rose, headline account performance can hide deterioration in the relevant goal. A good IPS review supports revising contributions and objectives instead of declaring success from a positive market chart."],"worked":["Account begins $10,000 and grows to $11,000 ⇒ first subperiod +10%.","New external contribution $9,000 raises account to $20,000.","End account $18,000 ⇒ second subperiod −10%.","Time-weighted linked return 1.10×.90−1=−1%.","Money-weighted result depends on exact cash-flow timing and can differ from linked return."],"practice":[["Why is the S&P 500 alone not always the correct benchmark for a portfolio holding bonds and cash?","The policy's benchmark should reflect its asset mix and risk purpose, so comparing unlike exposures can exaggerate apparent failure or success."],["Why can a household's money-weighted return differ from a fund's time-weighted performance report?","The household contributes and withdraws cash at specific dates, which changes how many dollars participate in each period's price change."]]},{"title":"Behavioral guardrails, conflicts and emergency procedures","body":["Markets create predictable pressure on human behavior: loss aversion, overconfidence after gains, recency bias, herding and reluctance to sell a declining concentrated position. An investment policy does not eliminate emotion, but it can replace decisions made during panic with precommitted procedures. A household should specify how long to evaluate a major change, what evidence must be assembled and which urgent events justify immediate action. No policy can promise that investors will always follow it.","Risk questionnaires are useful starting evidence but can be biased toward products offered by their sponsor, as Investor.gov notes. Advice compensation, commissions, proprietary products and recurring account fees may create conflicts. An IPS should document who advises the household, which services are paid, the full fee stack, how product recommendations are assessed, and who retains final authority over execution. Independent verification matters especially for annuities, private investments and complex trust-linked financial products.","Define an emergency protocol: if essential reserve falls below $6,000, suspend discretionary investments and calculate replenishment; if a home closing date moves forward, verify settlement and accessible balance; if an employer income disruption occurs, reassess contributions and portfolio loss capacity; if one holding breaches issuer cap, review tax-aware trimming. This is not a promise that the app can automatically execute trades. These are decisions for the investor and qualified professionals.","Revisions should have a change-control section: revision date, changed assumptions, new policy, responsible signatories and next review date. An investment professional may recommend a change, but signed contracts, trust instruments and brokerage authorizations remain controlling. The objective is operational clarity without claiming a personal note can override legal or financial restrictions."],"worked":["Emergency cash floor $6,000; accessible cash falls to $4,500 after a repair.","Reserve deficit $1,500 triggers pause-and-replenish review under this fictional policy.","At $250 savings per month, six full contributions replenish $1,500 if no other flows.","A self-imposed maximum 10% issuer allocation requires checking actual combined fund and direct holdings.","IPS revisions require documented change in circumstances, not a guaranteed market timing signal."],"practice":[["Why is it useful to write an emergency investment suspension rule before job loss or market panic?","The household can respond to a measurable liquidity fact rather than trying to decide risk appetite while pressured by fear and immediate bills."],["How can an investment-product seller's compensation affect an IPS review?","Commissions and proprietary-product incentives can introduce conflicts; fees, alternatives and independent sources must be disclosed and considered."]]},{"title":"The investment committee's completed IPS and monitoring dashboard","body":["A final IPS should read like an implementation document. Start with household purpose, asset inventory and liabilities. State mandatory minimum cash, each goal's funding and time horizon, risk capacity and willingness, expected contributions and tax account restrictions. Provide target allocation, allowed ranges, prohibited leverage and private-product limits, brokerage custody standards and evidence for security selection. Include benchmarks, performance method, rebalancing and review cadence, emergency triggers, recordkeeping and approval signatures.","One integrated fictional example protects $6,000 emergency and $12,000 closing cash from the $18,000 accessible household pool, while using a separate $40,000 long-run pool for 60/30/10 strategic exposure. The household should never display the same dollars as an emergency fund and a freely investable retirement asset. New $250 monthly investment contributions are conditional on bills and reserve remaining funded; no hypothetical future paycheck is counted as cash in hand.","Quantify the long-run downside. With $24,000 equities at −35%, $12,000 bonds at −8% and $4,000 cash unchanged, ending account is $15,600+$11,040+$4,000=$30,640, a $9,360 or 23.4% drawdown. State whether the household can accept that without selling to pay current obligations. If not, revise targets, contribution plan, or risk capacity. A formal IPS makes clear how losses are measured and what would trigger a reconsideration.","Conclude with an academic committee vote: approve subject to constraints, reject until cash is funded, or request documents. A robust statement anticipates a sudden repair, a job interruption, a large market decline and a beneficiary transition. It should be easy enough for a partner or trustee to follow, yet rigorous enough for another analyst to reproduce all amounts and evaluate conflicts and legal uncertainties."],"worked":["Separate immediate cash reserve $6,000 and home fund $12,000 = $18,000 protected liquidity.","Long-run $40,000 pool at 60/30/10 = stock $24,000, bonds $12,000, cash $4,000.","Shock stocks −35% ⇒ $15,600; bonds −8% ⇒ $11,040; cash $4,000.","Stressed long-run balance $30,640; loss $9,360 =23.4%.","New $250/month contribution remains conditional on actual household surplus."],"practice":[["What makes the final IPS usable by a partner or trustee who did not help design it?","It records concrete amounts, ownership and access rights, account roles, permitted risk, funding triggers and responsible decision-makers with a dated revision process."],["What is the strongest test that an IPS is fit for the household rather than only cosmetically complete?","Stress the plan against simultaneous market loss and essential cash demands and confirm it can still meet obligations without relying on undocumented assets or guaranteed recovery."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["CFA 2026 Basics Portfolio Planning and Construction","https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/basics-of-portfolio-planning-and-construction"],["CFA Institute Suitability Standard III(C)","https://www.cfainstitute.org/standards/professionals/code-ethics-standards/standards-of-practice-iii-c"],["Investor.gov Asset Allocation and Rebalancing","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"],["Investor.gov Investor Risk Tolerance","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-risk"],["CFPB Buying a House","https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/"],["IRS Publication 550 Taxable Investment Income","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"]],"assessment":{"title":"Fully documented personal IPS and governance capstone","prompt":"Draft a signed-approval-format IPS for a fictional Connecticut family with $18,000 restricted near-term cash ($6,000 emergency, $12,000 closing), $40,000 separately investable long-term funds and conditional $250 monthly savings. Compute a 60/30/10 mix, bands 55–65% equities, maximum 10% issuer concentration, six-month review and threshold policy. Test stocks −35%, bonds −8% and no cash change. Evaluate $20,000 goal after 24 deposits from $12,000 start, classify risk willingness versus capacity, and state time-weighted versus money-weighted return distinction on $10k→$11k +$9k deposit→$18k scenario.","rubric":["IPS objective and liability specificity (20%)","Capacity, liquidity and tax/legal constraints (25%)","Allocation, drift, scenario arithmetic (25%)","Governance, performance evaluation and exception rules (20%)","Documented primary evidence and professional limitations (10%)"]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Connecticut Wealth and Homeownership: Institutional Portfolio Committee","intro":"Fictional college-style portfolio construction and investment policy exercise. Figures are modeled, not live quotes, guaranteed market results or personal legal advice. Analyze both asset returns and liability cash dates, and justify the operating rules for a future household trustee or partner.","scenario":["A household has $18,000 accessible cash allocated entirely to an immediately needed $6,000 emergency reserve and $12,000 planned house closing twelve months later. A possible $2,500 unexpected car repair and temporary employment interruption must be considered. The family wishes to support a daughter eventually through inherited property but no signed trust or beneficiary terms are specified.","A separate long-horizon portfolio contains $40,000 genuinely not needed for home or emergencies. One allocation illustration is 70/20/10 stocks/bonds/cash; a separate formal investment policy illustration uses 60/30/10. Different cases must not be blended without naming them.","The risk committee is examining stock expected return 7%, bond expected return 3%, cash expected return 2% with stock annual standard deviation 20%, bond annual standard deviation 8%, and correlation .25. The 60/40 two-asset risk analysis intentionally excludes cash and uses weights 60% stocks and 40% bonds.","Three illustrative market states for the 60/40 portfolio are growth (+8% stocks,+3% bonds), recession (−35% stocks,+5% bonds) and inflation shock (−25% stocks,−12% bonds). Treat these as assumed return shocks without probabilities, not current market predictions.","A separate retirement account has drifted to stocks $8,000 and bonds $4,000 after initial 60/40 targets. Policy absolute drift threshold is five percentage points. External new contributions can be directed toward underweight bonds, but available monthly savings are only $250 if income continues.","A taxable stock tax lot can be sold for $800 with properly identified basis $500. Assume a conditional illustrative 15% federal tax rate on eligible recognized long-term gain, excluding actual state and other tax rules. Fund operating and advisory fees total roughly 1% annually under a simplified steady model.","Another retirement account originally holds $12,000 stocks and $8,000 bonds; equities fall 30%, bonds rise 5%. The committee considers rebalancing, while a hypothetical new essential $3,000 cash requirement may instead change permissible targets.","A long-horizon $40,000 pool is proposed at 60% equities, 30% high-quality bonds and 10% cash. Stress equities −35%, bonds −8% and cash unchanged. The outside $18,000 protected emergency/closing funds must not be used to make the downside portfolio result look better.","A goal requires $20,000 available in 24 months; dedicated funds are currently $12,000 and current affordable deposits are $250 monthly, assuming zero investment return. Interpret the funded gap and savings requirement honestly.","A portfolio concentration example holds a single company at 35% of a $20,000 investment pool; assume that company drops 60%, while other assets are unchanged. Separately two equal funds each hold a common issuer group at 40% and 60% respectively.","A fictional one-year portfolio with expected return 8%, risk-free proxy 2% and volatility 15% is used to model Sharpe ratio. For a separate normal-distribution VaR illustration, mean 5%, volatility 15%, lower 5% standard normal z=−1.645. Quantiles are not maximum losses.","Performance assessment uses a three-step history: account begins $10,000, grows to $11,000, receives an additional external $9,000, then ends at $18,000. Use this data to compute the linked time-weighted performance across the two subperiods, not to infer a known money-weighted return absent dates."],"tasks":["Identify immediate and dated liabilities, verify the $18,000 pool has no unrestricted surplus, and calculate how a $2,500 essential repair affects accessible cash and the funding gap.","Apply a 70/20/10 mix to the separate $40,000 long-run pool; compute exposure and adverse returns when equities fall 35%, bonds 5%, cash holds steady.","Distinguish willingness, financial risk capacity, required target return and risk tolerance with a plausible income loss scenario; state objective changes needed if goal funding is insufficient.","Compute the 60/40 two-asset expected return and variance/standard deviation from σstock=20%, σbond=8% and correlation .25; stress the covariance assumption at negative and higher positive correlations.","Evaluate the three hypothetical 60/40 macro market outcomes; explain why a backtested correlation is not a recession cash-flow guarantee.","Quantify concentrated issuer and overlapping fund exposures, including job income and housing collateral as correlated household risks.","Compute Sharpe ratio and a lower 5% normal VaR loss threshold, then state explicitly what VaR fails to bound.","Calculate actual asset weights from $8,000 stocks and $4,000 bonds, the full 60/40 rebalancing trade, the 5-percentage-point trigger and contribution-only correction.","Compute conditional recognized capital gain and federal tax using $800 sale proceeds, $500 basis and illustrative 15% rate; list lot, state tax, fee and wash-sale unknowns.","Calculate weights and trades after a $20,000 starting 60/40 portfolio has stocks fall 30% and bonds rise 5%; explain changes under a simultaneous $3,000 withdrawal requirement.","Apply a 60/30/10 strategic mix to $40,000, compute a simultaneous −35% equity and −8% bond stress, and evaluate the effect on the protected separate cash pool.","Calculate the zero-return contribution required to reach $20,000 from $12,000 in twenty-four months, compare with $250 current deposits and explicitly record the shortfall.","Calculate time-weighted subperiod return using $10k→$11k, then $9k contribution and $20k→$18k; distinguish money-weighted return limitations.","Draft a full IPS with goals, constraints, account roles, fund selection rules, allowed allocation bands, max issuer concentration, no-margin guardrail, taxable execution policy, fee budget, benchmarks, review authority and revised-plan triggers."],"answers":["Accessible $18,000 fully allocated to $6,000 emergency and $12,000 home closing; surplus $0. An immediate $2,500 repair reduces balance to $15,500 and creates $2,500 shortfall against unchanged goals until replenished.","70/20/10 on $40k: equities $28k, bonds $8k, cash $4k. Stocks −35%=$18,200, bonds −5%=$7,600, cash $4k, total $29,800; loss $10,200=25.5%.","Risk willingness is emotional comfort; capacity is ability to fund liabilities after losses. A $20k target from $12k with 24 deposits at $250 yields $18k principal, creating $2k gap absent return; improve cash saving, adjust deadline or target rather than promise a high market return.","60/40 expectation .6×7%+.4×3%=5.4%. Variance .6²×.2²+.4²×.08²+2×.6×.4×.25×.2×.08=.017344; σ≈13.1697%. Different correlations alter covariance term and volatility.","Growth .6×8%+.4×3%=6%. Recession .6×(−35%)+.4×5%=−19%. Inflation shock .6×(−25%)+.4×(−12%)=−19.8%. These are scenario calculations, not actual forecasts.","Single company weight .35×$20,000=$7,000; issuer −60% loses $4,200=21% of entire portfolio. Equal overlapping funds: group exposure .5×40%+.5×60%=50% of total; employee wages and mortgage location may add hidden risk.","Sharpe (8−2)/15=0.4. Normal model lower 5% return quantile=5%−1.645×15%=−19.675%; on $20k corresponding loss≈$3,935. Actual losses can exceed this, particularly if return tails depart from normal assumptions.","$8k/$12k=66.6667% stocks; absolute drift 6.6667 percentage points above 60%; full trade sell $800 stock and buy $800 bonds. Contribution-only new bond cash x solves $8k/($12k+x)=60%, x≈$1,333.33, over five $250 contributions.","Recognized gain $800−$500=$300. At hypothetical eligible 15% federal rate, tax $45, excluding legal state, NIIT and actual holding-period conditions; track basis and trade documentation.","After stocks $12k×.70=$8,400 and bonds $8k×1.05=$8,400, total $16,800 at 50/50. Target stock 60%=$10,080, bond=$6,720; buy $1,680 equities with bonds if still suitable. A required $3k withdrawal may override target.","60/30/10 on $40k: stocks $24k, bonds $12k, cash $4k. Under −35%/−8%/0% shocks: $15,600+$11,040+$4,000=$30,640, loss $9,360=23.4%; outside $18k protected assets serve other obligations.","Target $20k less $12k=$8k. Over 24 zero-yield deposits requires $333.33/month. At $250 deposits, total new $6k and terminal principal $18k, leaving $2k unfilled.","Time-weighted portfolio growth first +10%; after $9k deposit, second subperiod falls $20k→$18k or −10%; linked (1.10×.90−1)=−1%. Money-weighted result depends on dated deposits and cannot be inferred without precise timing.","Committee recommendation specifies protected cash and a conditional long-run risk portfolio with measurable targets, stated constraints, tax and cost limits, authorized governance, documented exceptions and at least semiannual review; no implicit guaranteed stock returns or trust/mortgage permissions."],"rubric":["Household liabilities, three buckets and suitability (25%)","Covariance and market scenario math (25%)","Rebalancing, costs and tax-aware trade sizing (20%)","Institutional IPS, governance and monitoring (20%)","Primary citations, uncertainties and ethical representation (10%)"],"sources":[["CFA 2026 Portfolio Planning and Construction","https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/basics-of-portfolio-planning-and-construction"],["CFA Suitability and IPS Standard","https://www.cfainstitute.org/standards/professionals/code-ethics-standards/standards-of-practice-iii-c"],["Investor.gov Asset Allocation","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"],["SEC Rebalancing Guide","https://www.sec.gov/investor/pubs/assetallocation.htm"],["IRS Investment Income","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"],["IRS Cost Basis","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p551"]]},"rules":{"preserveOriginalChapters":true,"preserveProgressKey":true,"hypotheticalRiskAssumptions":true,"noAccreditationClaim":true,"localInputNotSaved":true}};
